<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Lithuanian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Lithuanian War was an undeclared conflict of 1920 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> region. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwalki">Suwałki</a> Agreement, the Polish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Żeligowski</a> seized Vilnius in October 1920 in a staged mutiny; Lithuanian forces then halted his advance at Širvintos and Giedraičiai, and a ceasefire in late November left Vilnius in Polish hands, poisoning relations between the two states until 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-war.jpg" alt="Polish–Lithuanian War" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soldiers_of_the_Lithuanian_Army_in_Vilnius,_Lithuania,_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Soviet Russia competed for control of overlapping territories. The conflict between the first two centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> had declared the capital of the restored Lithuanian state. The Polish Army seized the city on 19 April 1919, and the first clashes between Polish and Lithuanian soldiers took place near Vievis in the spring of that year. The Entente drew demarcation lines that neither side accepted, and the front then stabilised until the summer of 1920, when the Polish-Soviet War turned against Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 1920 Polish forces retreated before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> moved to secure territory the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> left behind. The Soviets entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> first, on 14 July, and handed it to the Lithuanians on 26 August after their defeat in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. Poland attacked Lithuanian units in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwalki">Suwałki</a> region on 22 September. Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, it signed the Suwałki Agreement on 7 October 1920, which drew an incomplete demarcation line that left Vilnius open to a flanking move.</p><p>On 8 October the Polish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Żeligowski</a> staged a mutiny, secretly planned and authorised by the Polish chief of state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>. His division of 14,000 soldiers took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> one day before the agreement was to take effect, against only two Lithuanian battalions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> officially called Żeligowski a deserter but supplied his units. On 12 October he proclaimed the Republic of Central Lithuania, with Vilnius as its capital. His offensive towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> was halted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, and a ceasefire was signed on 29 November 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Republic of Central Lithuania was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as the Wilno Voivodeship in 1922, and in March 1923 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a> recognised the armistice line as the Polish-Lithuanian border. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> held in 1931 that Poland had broken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> by occupying Vilnius. Lithuania, which had defended its independence but lost a third of its territory and its capital, kept Vilnius as its constitutional capital and broke off relations with Poland until a Polish ultimatum in March 1938. It regained the city on 28 October 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Lithuanian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
